
[[cpp]]
=== {cpp}

{cpp} has no language-level package manager, so a {cpp} pool's dependencies are
external *system libraries*: the environment provisions the library (its headers
and shared objects) and the pool links against it.

==== Library dependencies (`cpp-deps`)

Declare the libraries a module needs in its `package.yaml` under `cpp-deps`,
using `package: version-constraint`:

[source, yaml]
----
cpp-deps:
  boost: ">=1.80"
----

`cpp-deps` are provisioned from the https://conda-forge.org/[conda-forge]
package database -- installing the library's headers and shared objects into the
environment. `conda` is the default (and only) source, so the bare form above is
equivalent to `boost: {version: ">=1.80", source: conda}`. The compiler takes
the union across every imported module and the solver intersects conflicting
constraints.

A `cpp-deps` entry may also name a conda `channel` other than conda-forge (see
<<py-deps>> for the full rules), for a library that lives on a
subordinate channel. A program your {cpp} code runs, rather than a library it
links, is declared under `tool-deps` (<<tool-deps>>).

==== Headers and link names (`import`, `libs`)

An entry can also name the headers your code includes and the libraries the
pool links against:

[source, yaml]
----
cpp-deps:
  htslib:
    version: ">=1.20"
    import: htslib/sam.h
    libs: [hts]
----

Each name under `libs` becomes a linker flag: `hts` becomes `-lhts`. Write the
bare name, without `-l`, a `lib` prefix or a file suffix. The link name sits on
the package that provides it because the two names often differ: the `htslib`
package provides `libhts`, and `boost` provides `boost_filesystem` and
`boost_system`. A header-only library needs no `libs`.

`import` takes one header or a list. Morloc checks that every header a
program's modules declare can be found, and a missing one stops the build with
an error that names the package and the header, such as `C++ dependency htslib
(header htslib/sam.h) not found`. This check covers headers only. A missing
library still fails at link time, with the linker's `cannot find -lhts`.

In a managed environment, Morloc adds the environment's `include` and `lib`
directories to every {cpp} pool build and records the `lib` directory as the
pool's run path. The build does not depend on the compiler's own search paths,
and the program runs from any shell, as long as the environment stays where it
was built.

For any other compiler flags -- `-DFOO`, `-O3`, `-march=native` -- use
`cxx-flags`. Each entry reaches the compiler as one argument, so
`'-DMSG="a b"'` stays one define. A library the environment does not provide,
such as the system's zlib or one your setup script builds, is linked through
`cxx-flags` too: `cxx-flags: ["-lz"]`. These fields propagate transitively
through a module's dependencies.

Older modules listed link names under a `dependencies` field. That field is
gone, and Morloc refuses a `package.yaml` that still has it.

The `local-deps` section that Python and Rust use for in-project packages
(<<local-deps>>) is not available for {cpp}, which has no package manager to
track such an install; a library you build yourself is linked through
`cxx-flags`.

[[cpp-runtime-helpers]]
==== Runtime helpers (`cppmorloc_api.hpp`)

The {cpp} binding's helpers (<<runtime-helpers>>) are declared in
`cppmorloc_api.hpp`, which every {cpp} pool includes before your headers. Each
helper defines a feature macro; test it, so the header also builds outside a
pool.

`mlc::give(f, std::move(x)...)` calls the function value `f` and hands it
ownership of its arguments, which you must not read again. Calling `f(x)`
directly lends `x`, so a function value that keeps or reshapes its argument
copies it; `give` lets one that can take the value, such as a lambda passing it
on at its last use, move it instead. A loop that pulls a batch from a stream
and passes it to a function value is the usual place for it:

[source, cpp]
----
template <class F>
inline auto giveTo(F f, int n) {
    std::vector<int> b;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) b.push_back(i);
#ifdef MLC_GIVE
    return mlc::give(f, std::move(b));
#else
    return f(std::move(b));
#endif
}
----

Where `f` has no such form, `give` calls it as usual and the argument is lent.


